<Project>

  <!--
    One language version for every project under desktop/, set here and nowhere else.

    Measured on 2026-11-16 before this file existed: none of the seven csproj named LangVersion, so
    each compiled at whatever the SDK defaults to for its target framework and nothing in the
    tree said which version that was. This file makes the number explicit in one place; a project
    added tomorrow inherits it instead of silently compiling at a different default.

    WHY 12.0, AND IT IS A REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. The desktop already uses C# 12:
    `GameBuddy.Desktop/ChildStderrCapture.cs:100` declares `private sealed class
    BoundedBuffer(int capacity)` - a primary constructor - and
    `CurrentUserRootLayout.cs`/`DeploymentIdentity.cs` build collection expressions. So this line
    does not merely pin a default: without it the tree would depend on the net8.0 default
    (C# 12) to compile at all, which is why `dotnet publish -p:LangVersion=10.0` fails with
    `error CS8936: feature 'primary constructor' is not available in C# 10.0`. The dependency
    was real and undeclared; this file declares it.

    WHY NOT 13.0. The ceiling is the pinned compiler, not a preference. global.json pins SDK
    8.0.424 (rollForward latestPatch -> 8.0.425), whose Roslyn accepts up to C# 12; asking for
    13.0 fails every project in this tree with `error CS1617: invalid option "13.0" for
    /langversion`. A language version the installed compiler rejects is a build break, so this
    line is verified by building, not by reading. Raise it only after global.json selects a newer
    SDK.

    WHY NO POLYSHARP. The only C# 13 feature that needs a runtime type is `params` collections
    (`System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ParamCollectionAttribute`), and no project here declares
    one. A package that backfills an unused feature is a dependency that buys nothing; it gets
    re-evaluated together with the SDK pin, which is the same line that would unlock 13.0.

    NOT SET HERE: TargetFramework. Each desktop project owns its own, and
    `tools/check-dotnet-target-and-language.mjs` is what keeps them honest.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
